@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  task, task id, brief, child run, result, domain event, child port,
  child run state, enqueue time, start time, child driver, transcript, attach command,
  launch spec, model listing, model check, prerequisites check, starter tools,
  follow-up message, delivery outcome, turn, turn number,
}
@design/cli.sigil from Cli import { subzero init }
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, project root, load error, tool list,
  unresolved root error, model, parallel limit, general agent,
  model tier, tier error, agent file, timeout, fallback agent,
  working directory, medium tier, setup state, change set, setup preview,
  preview fingerprint, change set error, stale preview error, write error,
  refused change, setup scope, merged configuration, off error, global file,
  enabled switch, built-in agent, child host options, endpoints entry,
  agent entry,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  task record, task status, parent session id, start-up sweep, task index,
  owner process, collected marker,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  driver registry, parent adapter, supported child host, child host, parent host,
  parent model,
}

component DelegationService {
  goal {
    Give every *front end* one entry that is ready to delegate: it turns a
    named
    agent, a model tier or neither, and a brief into a task, finds a live task
    by its task id later, sends a task of the same parent session a
    follow-up message, and starts clean after an earlier process has died.
  }

  state {
    A *delegation* is the request of a front end. It holds an optional agent
    name, an optional model tier, a *brief text*, optional files, an optional
    expected output, an optional *reply budget* and an optional
    parent session id.
    The reply budget is the number of characters that the child's final reply
    should stay under. Only a front end that knows how its parent host receives
    a
    result gives one, and DelegationService holds no size of its own.

    The *live task registry* holds the tasks that this process has started or
    continued, by their task id and by the parent session id of their
    delegation.

    A *continuation* is the request of a front end to send a follow-up message
    to a task. It holds a task id, the follow-up message, an optional wait and
    the parent session id of its caller.

    A *continuation queue* holds the continuations of one task in this
    process, in the order received.

    A *notice sink* is the one entry that each front end registers with
    DelegationService, to which DelegationService offers the completion
    notice of a turn that no call of that front end opened: a turn opened
    for held follow-up messages, for unread steering messages or for a
    replay.

    A *wait handle* is what a front end holds while a delegation, a
    continuation or a wait up to a bound is waited for. It takes an *until signal*,
    which ends the wait and returns the current state of the task,
    and an abort, which cancels, and the two are separate. A *forced wait*
    is a wait that a front end asks for because nothing would collect a
    result after its process exits, as in a print mode or a non-interactive
    session.

    A turn is collected once any path has delivered its result to the parent
    model, whether a collect, a waited reply or a delivery that a front end
    makes by its own means, and every such path writes the collected marker
    of that turn.

    A *session cancel* cancels every Pending and live task of one
    parent session id in the live task registry, and reports their task ids and
    how many there were.

    A *collect tool* is the tool of a front end that returns the result of a
    task that its caller did not wait for.

    A *completion notice* tells a parent model that a turn of one of its tasks
    settled:
    it
    names
    the task by its task id with its status and, from turn 2 on, its
    turn number, tells the parent model to collect
    the
    result through the collect tool that the front end names, lists the tasks of
    the same parent session id other than its own task that are still Pending or
    live with their status line, and tells the parent model to wait for them before
    it uses the
    results
    together. It carries no result text, and it names no running tasks and
    gives
    no instruction to wait when none are running. A *stop notice* is the text
    for
    one task that was stopped, through a session cancel or a cancel, and it
    says that the task was stopped and that no action is needed. A
    session cancel
    gives one stop notice for each task it stopped.

    A *queued task* is a task whose child run is Pending in this process, or a
    task
    of another process whose task status is running and that has no start time.
    Queued is a view that DelegationService derives on each reply from the live
    task and the task record, and it is never a stored task status.

    The *queue place* of a child run whose child run state is Pending is its
    position, counted from 1, among the Pending child runs of its
    model in this process, in the order they arrived.

    A *status line* reports a task whose latest turn has no result yet. For a
    queued task of
    this process it gives the model, the queue place and the time
    waited since the enqueue time, and for a queued task of another process it
    gives the model and the time waited with no queue place:
    ```text
    queued, not launched yet: no child is running; it starts when model <m>
    has room (place <n> in this session's queue, waiting <elapsed>)
    ```
    For a task whose child run has a start time the status line gives that
    start time and how long ago it was:
    ```text
    running, started <iso> (<elapsed> ago)
    ```

    The *result text* of a settled turn starts with a line that holds the
    task id
    first, then the agent, the turn number from turn 2 on, the task status and
    the failure kind when there is one. Lines follow for the message when there is
    one, the summary, with
    (empty) for an empty summary, and the usage with the duration when
    the result holds usage. A notice goes on the line after the first one, so
    the task id still comes first. A result whose held follow-up messages opened a
    new turn ends with one line that says so.

    The *guided setup* is the part of DelegationService that a front end
    reaches during an init that a person starts. It is made with Kernel, the
    project root that a DelegationService resolved and an optional child host,
    and it offers five *setup operations*, which list the models, show the
    setup state, check models, preview a change set and apply it. Its
    child host is the one named, or else the one supported child host when the
    driver registry lists exactly one. Otherwise it refuses with a message that
    names the supported child hosts, or that no child host is registered when
    the driver registry lists none.

    The *guide text* is the one conversation, its steps, questions,
    suggestions and warnings, that every parent adapter follows over the
    setup operations during an init that a person starts. It never
    writes a parallel limit for a model of an endpoints entry that the person
    did not confirm. After it shows the
    setup state it asks whether the chosen models are for every project or
    only this one, which gives the setup scope of its preview and apply. It
    asks the parallel limit of each chosen model and, when the setup state
    holds an endpoints entry, of every model in its list, chosen or not,
    offering the parallel limit of that entry for each, before the write that
    removes the endpoints map, and
    before the confirmation it says which model tier or limit of the change
    set this project overrides when the setup scope is the global file. After the
    stale preview error of an apply it previews the change set again, and
    after any other setup refusal of an apply it ends the init, writing
    nothing. When an endpoints entry of the setup state is in the file that
    the chosen setup scope does not write, the guide text first repairs that
    file: it previews a change set for the setup scope of that file, which
    sets the parallel limit of each model in the list of its endpoints entry,
    offering the parallel limit of that entry, and removes its endpoints map,
    and applies it only after its own confirmation. Then it goes on with the
    chosen setup scope.
  }

  logic {
    On start-up, ask AgentConfig for the project root from the roots or the
    fallback working directory that the front end gives. When no
    project root resolves, the start-up waits, and each later call resolves it
    again and returns the unresolved root error to its caller, with no task
    made, until one resolves. A front end may ask for the project root to be
    resolved from the roots it reports alone, as a parent host does that
    reports
    one working directory as its only root. Once a project root resolves,
    keep it for
    the service, run the start-up sweep with it, and connect TaskCore to the
    task record, given that project root. The start-up ends when the sweep has
    finished and TaskCore is connected.

    On a delegation, load the merged configuration again, giving AgentConfig
    each supported child host that the driver registry lists and the starter
    tools of the child driver of the child host of the medium tier, which
    fill each built-in agent. When the load fails, return the load error to
    the caller, or the off error when the project is off, and end the flow
    with no task made. First choose the agent. When the delegation names no agent
    or
    names the agent
    general, take the general agent. When it names another agent, look up that
    name among the agent entries of the merged configuration, a built-in
    agent included. A name that finds the fallback agent in that lookup
    runs it and reports nothing about a name not configured. When the name is
    unknown, take the fallback agent and report in the reply that the named
    agent
    is not configured. Then choose the model tier: when the delegation names
    one,
    drop a leading tier: from it, and ask AgentConfig for the agent definition
    of
    the chosen agent at that model tier, or else at the agent's own tier or the
    medium tier. When AgentConfig returns the tier error for that agent, return
    it
    to the caller and end the flow with no task made. Take the child host
    options for the child host of that agent definition from the same load.
    The task records its
    agent as
    the name of the chosen agent, followed by :<tier> when the delegation names
    a
    model tier.

    With the agent definition found, ask the driver registry for the child port
    of
    its child host, and when no child driver is registered for that child host,
    return the
    error of the driver registry to the caller and end the flow with no task
    made.
    A driver registry that lists no supported child host gives AgentConfig an
    empty
    list
    of supported child hosts, so a request that resolves to any tier gives the
    tier error that names the child host and lists no supported child host,
    before
    the
    driver registry is asked, and no task is made.
    Otherwise render the files, the expected output and the reply budget after
    the brief text into one brief, start the task in TaskCore with that
    agent definition, that brief, that child port, those child host options
    and the parent session id,
    and add the task to the live task registry, which a later collect or
    cancel reads. When the child runs of its model in this process
    that are Starting or Live have reached its parallel limit, keep the
    child run Pending at the next queue place and release it, first come first
    served, when one of them ends, and otherwise release it at once. When the
    caller waits, offer each domain event to the caller and return the result
    when the child run settles, which ends the flow. When the caller does not
    wait, return the task id at once with the start time of a released
    child run, or with the model, the queue place and the
    enqueue time of a Pending one, which ends the flow.

    On a continuation, first register its task id in the live task registry as
    opening, before its first wait, so that a session cancel that arrives during
    the sweep or the load of the merged configuration stops the turn before it
    starts, and the continuation then ends with the delivery outcome not delivered
    and the reason cancelled. Hand the continuation to the continuation queue of
    its task, which runs the continuations of one task one at a time. Then run the
    start-up sweep again, then look for its task in
    the live task registry and then in the task record, and read its
    owner process and its latest turn from the task record in both cases. A
    task in the live task registry whose owner process is now another process
    is dropped from the live task registry and handled as a task that is not
    there, so that its turns are read again from the task record. A task id
    that no
    task record holds gets an error that names it, which ends the flow. A task
    whose parent session id is not the parent session id of the continuation, and a
    task whose latest turn is
    running in an owner process other than this one that is still alive get
    the delivery outcome
    not delivered with the status line of the task, or the first line of its
    result text when its latest turn has settled, and no result, which ends
    the flow. A continuation with no parent session id, and a continuation for a
    task that has none, get the delivery outcome not delivered with the message
    that this conversation could not be identified, never the status line or the
    result of the task, which ends the flow. A task whose task status is abandoned
    is continued as a settled
    one. For a task of the same parent session id that is not in the
    live task registry, load the merged configuration as for a delegation,
    take the child port of the child host of its frozen agent definition from
    the driver registry and the child host options for that child host from
    the same load, return a load error, the off error or an error of the
    driver registry as a delegation does, with nothing sent, and otherwise add
    the task to the live task registry. Then hand the follow-up message to
    TaskCore. With the delivery outcome queued or steered, return it with the
    status line of the task, which ends the flow. With the delivery outcome
    new turn, keep the child run of the new turn Pending and release it under
    the parallel limit of its model as for a delegation, and when the caller
    waits, offer each domain event and return the result of that turn when it
    settles, and otherwise return the delivery outcome new turn with the
    status line at once, which ends the flow. With the delivery outcome
    not delivered from TaskCore, return it with a message that the child host
    of the task cannot take a follow-up message, which ends the flow. A new turn
    that TaskCore opens for held follow-up messages is released under the
    parallel limit of its model in the same way, and its result reaches the
    front end through the completion notice, which ends that flow. Only a
    continuation that opens a new turn, whether as a new turn or as a release,
    loads the merged configuration, before it claims the turn and just before its
    child run enters the queue of its model, and takes the parallel limit of that
    model from it, whether its task came from the live task registry or from its
    task record. A continuation that steers or holds loads none and uses the frozen
    task record, so that a broken agent file never blocks steering a running child.
    A continuation whose steering finds the child run already settled opens a new
    turn, so it loads the merged configuration just before it claims that turn.
    When that load fails, the continuation ends with the delivery outcome not
    delivered and the load error or the off error, and no turn is claimed. A
    process that loses the claim of a new turn to another process reads the
    task record again, and then holds, steers or answers not delivered as the
    latest turn there says.

    A delegation with no parent session id adds one line to its reply that
    says the task cannot be continued, because no conversation could name
    it.

    A waited delegation, a waited continuation and a wait up to a bound each
    end when their turn settles, when their bound passes or when their until
    signal fires, which returns the current state of the task and cancels
    nothing, and only the abort of the wait handle cancels. A forced wait
    also waits for each turn that the held follow-up messages of the awaited
    turn open, and returns each result in order, which ends that flow.

    Before a collect, a cancel or a listing, run the start-up sweep again, so
    that a task whose owner process died after start-up is not shown as
    running, and drop from the live task registry each task whose
    owner process is now another process, so that it is read from the
    task record with its latest turn. On a collect or a cancel by task id, look in
    the
    live task registry and then in the task record, which gives the stored
    result of a settled task or the task status of an abandoned one. A task
    with a stored result for its latest turn gives that result, and a task
    whose task status is abandoned gives no result and says that it is
    abandoned, which ends the flow. A collect of a task in the live task registry
    that has not settled
    gives no result and returns its start time, or its model,
    queue place and enqueue time while it is Pending, which ends the flow. A
    cancel of a task that has already settled returns its stored result
    unchanged, which ends the flow. A cancel of a Pending child run removes it
    from the queue of its model, and each later Pending child run
    moves up one queue place, which ends the flow. A collect of a task whose
    task status is running and that is not in the live task registry gives no
    result, returns its model, its enqueue time and its start time
    when one is recorded, read from the task record, and says that the task
    belongs to another process, which ends the flow. A cancel of such a task
    reports that it belongs to another process and cannot be cancelled here,
    and changes nothing, which ends the flow.

    On a collect, an await or the collect hint of a completion notice for a
    task, give the result of each turn that has a result and no collected
    marker, oldest first, then the status line of the latest turn when it
    has no result yet, and mark each turn given as collected, so that a
    restarted or taking-over process neither repeats nor skips a turn, which
    ends the flow. A front end that decides afterwards which of the results it
    shows, as an await reply with a size limit does, asks for the collect
    without the marking and marks the turns it showed, so that a result it left
    out is still uncollected.

    On a session cancel for a parent session id, cancel each Pending and live
    task of that parent session id in the live task registry the way a cancel
    does, and return their task ids and count, which ends the flow. A
    session cancel with no parent session id cancels nothing and returns that
    the session is unknown, which ends the flow.

    When a turn that its caller did not wait for settles, build its
    completion notice from the task id, the status and the turn number of the
    turn, the name of the
    collect tool of the front end, and the status line of each task of
    the same parent session id that is still Pending or live, and offer it to
    the front end that opened that turn, which is the front end of the
    delegation for turn 1, of the continuation for a later turn, and of this
    process for a turn opened for held follow-up messages, for unread steering
    messages or for a replay, through its notice sink, which ends that
    flow. A task that a
    session cancel or a cancel ended gets the stop notice instead.

    On a listing of the models in the guided setup, run the
    prerequisites check of the child driver of its child host when it offers
    one, and return its message as the error when it gives one. Then take the
    child host options for that child host
    from AgentConfig, also when the project is off, and return the load error as
    the error of the whole listing when that read fails. Otherwise return
    the model listing of that child driver for them, or an error when it offers
    none or
    the listing fails, which ends the flow. On a show, read the setup state
    from AgentConfig, marking each model tier listed or not from the
    model listing when the listing succeeds and otherwise giving the reason it
    failed, and return it with the warning of what configuring the model tier
    large moves off the parent host, which ends the flow. On a check, take the
    child host options for that child host from AgentConfig
    in the same way, and return the load error as the error of the whole check
    when that read fails. Otherwise run the
    model check of that child driver, given them, for
    each model, one after another, and
    return one result for each model in the order asked, which ends the flow.
    On a preview or an apply, hand the change set and, for an apply, the
    preview fingerprint to AgentConfig, with that child host and the setup
    scope that the caller gives, which is the agent file when it gives none,
    and return the setup preview, which ends the flow.

    On shutdown, cancel every task in the live task registry, wait up to a
    deadline for their child runs to end, and record each task whose child run
    was then still running as cancelled, so that no task record stays running
    while its owner process lives on, which ends the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    DelegationService must create no task or task record when no child driver
    is
    registered for the child host of the agent definition, when AgentConfig
    returns
    the tier error, or when any agent entry fails to load, and it must load
    each agent entry again on each delegation.

    A delegation that names an agent other than general must find that agent in
    the
    agent entries of the merged configuration and run it at the model tier
    that the delegation names, else at its
    own tier or the medium tier, except that a name that no agent entry holds
    runs
    the fallback agent, and its replies report it queued or started like any
    other
    delegation.

    The brief that the service renders must start with the brief text
    unchanged,
    followed by a Files section, an Expected output section and a Reply size
    section, each only when it is present. The Reply size section asks the
    child
    to keep its final reply under the reply budget and says that the reply is
    returned to the parent model whole and never cut. A delegation with an
    empty
    brief text must be refused whatever its files hold, and the paths of its
    files are not checked.

    Every start-up of a front end, whether a parent adapter or a command,
    must go through
    the start-up of the service, so that the start-up sweep runs at every
    start-up.

    The project root must be resolved once successfully for each DelegationService,
    and the
    agent file, the task record and the child run must all use that same
    project root, which must not change once it is resolved. A process holds at
    most one DelegationService for each parent session at a time. A
    parent adapter
    that builds one DelegationService for each parent session shuts the
    old one down before it starts the next, and a parent adapter that keeps one
    DelegationService for its process ends a parent session with a
    session cancel.
    No other component
    may call for a project root for its own use, and each receives it from
    DelegationService, with one exception: a parent adapter that runs in a
    process
    where no DelegationService starts reads the merged configuration under the
    project root
    that its parent host reports, because it has no DelegationService to ask. A
    call
    made before the
    project root is resolved must return the unresolved root error and must
    create no task, task record or line of the task index.

    The live task registry must hold no state beyond this process, and the
    task record stays the only durable state of a task.

    A session cancel must reach only the tasks of this process, and a task of
    another process stays as it is.

    A completion notice and a stop notice must never carry the transcript of a
    child run, and the stop notice must never ask the parent model to act.

    A parallel limit must be counted for each model from each child run of
    this process on that model that is Starting or Live, whatever its
    model tier, and a child run that is Pending must not be counted.
    A child run that ends in any way, whether Settled, Failed or Cancelled,
    must stop being counted at once and release the next Pending child run. A
    delegation from a command line process or from another session is not
    counted, and nothing may signal or lock across processes to enforce it.

    The guided setup must write the file of its setup scope only through an
    apply of AgentConfig with the preview fingerprint of a setup preview, and
    must add no agent entry, and must use
    the project root that a DelegationService resolved and never the
    working directory of its process. It must run one model check at a time,
    so that a single model server is not overloaded, and it must make no
    delegation, task or task record, because the models it checks are not in
    the agent file yet.

    A child run that is Pending must be returned with its queue place and its
    enqueue time and never with a start time, and the time it waits must not
    count toward its timeout.

    Continuations of one task must run one at a time through its
    continuation queue, so that two continuations of a settled task open
    exactly one turn and the second steers or holds into it. An until signal
    must end a wait without cancelling any task or turn. Every path that
    delivers the result of a turn to the parent model must mark that turn
    collected through its collected marker, and a collect must never give a
    collected turn again nor skip an uncollected one.

    A continuation must reach only a task of its own parent session id and
    must never change the frozen agent definition of that task, and
    DelegationService must pass its follow-up message to TaskCore unchanged.
    A reply about a task that a continuation did not reach must never repeat
    its result.

    DelegationService depends on TaskCore for the task, on the driver registry
    of
    Kernel for the child port of the child host of an agent definition and,
    for the guided setup, for the model listing, the model check, the
    prerequisites check and the starter tools of a child host, on
    AgentConfig
    for the agent definition, the child host options, the general agent, the
    fallback agent, the
    tier error, the project root and the setup preview, and on TaskStore for
    the task record, the task status and the start-up sweep. It must not
    depend on any particular
    child driver or parent adapter, and it names no child host, because more of
    both will
    come and
    each plugs in through Kernel.
  }

  cases {
    A delegation with an unknown agent name runs the fallback agent, records
    its
    agent as fallback, and its reply says that the named agent is not
    configured. A
    delegation that names the agent general runs the general agent on the
    medium tier and records its agent as general.

    A delegation with an unknown agent name, in a project with a fallback
    agent, a built-in one included, runs the fallback agent with its own tool list,
    records its
    agent as fallback, and its reply says that the named agent is not
    configured. A
    delegation that names the agent fallback runs the same agent and records
    the
    same agent, and its reply does not say that a name is not configured.

    A call made before any project root is known returns the
    unresolved root error and writes nothing, and the first call after a
    project root becomes known runs the start-up sweep.

    A delegation while the agent file is malformed returns the load error and
    writes no task record.

    Two delegations to agents on one model whose parallel limit is 1, through
    two model tiers that both name it, start two child runs. The second stays
    Pending until the first ends,
    whether it settles, fails by timeout or is cancelled, and is then released,
    and a cancel of the second while it is Pending starts no child.

    Three delegations that do not wait, on a model whose
    parallel limit is 1, return the first with its start time and the second
    and third Pending at queue place 1 and 2. Cancelling the second moves the
    third to queue place 1.

    A delegation with the model tier medium starts the general agent on the
    child host,
    model and thinking level of that model tier and records the
    agent definition
    general:medium, and tier:medium gives the same task. A delegation with no
    agent
    and no model tier runs the general agent with its full tool list and
    records
    its agent as general, and a delegation that names the agent general and no
    model tier does the same.

    A delegation with a model tier that is not configured returns the
    tier error and
    writes no task record. A delegation that names the agent reviewer and the
    model tier large runs reviewer on large, with reviewer's tier override for
    large
    when
    it has one, and records reviewer:large, and a delegation that names
    reviewer and
    no model tier runs it on its own tier and records reviewer.

    A delegation with files and an expected output gives a brief that starts
    with
    the brief text, then lists the files and the expected output.

    A delegation with a reply budget of 48,000 characters gives a brief that
    ends with a Reply size section that names 48,000 characters, and a
    delegation with no reply budget gives a brief with no Reply size section.

    A collect of a task whose owner process died after the process started says
    that the task is abandoned and has no result, because the sweep ran again
    before the collect.

    A collect by task id after the process restarted returns the stored result
    of a
    settled task, and says that an abandoned task has no result.

    A cancel of a running task that another process owns reports that it
    belongs
    to another process, signals no process and leaves the task status running.

    With two live tasks and one Pending task of parent session id A and one
    live
    task of parent session id B, a session cancel for A cancels the three tasks
    of
    A, reports their three task ids, and leaves the task of B running. A
    session cancel with no parent session id cancels nothing and says that the
    session is unknown.

    With three tasks of one parent session id that the caller did not wait for,
    the first to settle gives a completion notice that names it and its status,
    says how to
    collect its result, lists the other two with their status line and
    gives the instruction to wait for them. A task
    ended by a session cancel gives the stop notice and no completion notice.

    A delegation in a project with no agent file and a global file that holds
    a medium tier runs the general agent as a built-in agent on the medium
    tier, with the starter tools of the child driver. A delegation in a project
    with no agent file and no medium tier in the global file returns the off
    error and makes no task, and says to run subzero init. A delegation in a
    project whose agent file holds an enabled switch of false returns the off
    error and makes no task, whatever the global file holds.

    A shutdown while a child run ignores its cancel past the deadline records
    that task as cancelled, and its task record does not stay running.

    A continuation for a live task of the same parent session id returns
    steered with its status line, and the task later settles with one result.
    A continuation for a task of that parent session id that settled returns
    new turn and, when the caller does not wait, its status line; when that
    turn settles, its completion notice names turn 2.

    A continuation for a task of another parent session id returns
    not delivered with the first line of its result text, and sends nothing to
    the child.

    A continuation for a settled task of the same parent session id whose
    owner process has exited, after a pi resume in a new pi process, adds the
    task to the
    live task registry, opens a new turn, and the task record names this
    process as its owner process.

    With one live child run on a model whose parallel limit is 1, a
    continuation that opens a new turn on another task of that model keeps
    the new child run Pending at queue place 1 until the first one ends.

    Turn 1 settles with a held follow-up message that opens turn 2 before
    turn 1 was collected: a collect returns the result of turn 1, the line
    that its held message opened turn 2 and the status line of turn 2, and a
    second collect after turn 2 settles returns only turn 2, also from a
    process that starts later. A continuation for a running task whose agent
    file now fails to load still steers it, and a continuation that would
    open a new turn then answers not delivered with the load error and
    claims no turn. A session cancel that arrives while a continuation loads
    the merged configuration stops its turn before it starts. A continuation
    with no parent session id answers that this conversation could not be
    identified, and a delegation with none says in its reply that the task
    cannot be continued.
  }

  interface {
    Accept a delegation with an optional agent name, an optional model tier and
    an optional reply budget, which the brief tells the child, refuse it when
    its brief text is empty, and return the result with the task id first once
    the child run settles, or return the task id at once when the caller does
    not wait, with the start time of a released child run,
    or the model, the queue place and the enqueue time of a Pending
    one. When the named agent is not configured and the fallback agent runs
    instead, return the report that the named agent is not configured with
    the task id, whether or not the caller waits.

    Accept a continuation and return the delivery outcome of its
    follow-up message, with the status line of the task for queued, steered
    and new turn when the caller does not wait, the result of the new turn
    when the caller waits for it, and for not delivered the status line or the
    first line of the result text and no result.

    Let each front end register one notice sink for the completion notice of
    a turn that no call of it opened, and give each waited delegation,
    continuation and wait up to a bound a wait handle whose until signal
    ends the wait without cancelling. Let a collect of a task by task id
    give each uncollected turn, oldest first, before the status line of its
    latest turn.

    Report each queued task by its model, by its queue place when it
    is a
    task of this process, and by the time it has waited, and never as started.
    Give every front end the same wording for it, the status line of a task
    that
    has no result and the result text of a settled task, so that no front end
    words the state of a task for itself.

    Offer each domain event of the child run of a waited delegation to the
    caller
    as it happens.

    Hand the project root, once resolved, to the front end that started the
    service, for that front end's own use, and never resolve a different one.

    Let a caller collect the result of the latest turn of a task by task id,
    returning no result
    while its task status is running, but its start time, or its
    model,
    queue place and enqueue time while it is Pending, and let a caller cancel a
    task by task id.

    Let a caller wait up to a bound for a task by task id, and answer as a
    collect
    does when the bound passes with the task still unsettled or queued.

    Give a front end each supported child host that the driver registry lists,
    so
    that
    it can describe each routable tier without being given a child driver.

    Give a front end the attach command of a settled task from its task record
    and the child driver registered for its child host. Refuse the attach
    command while the task is running or when its task record has no launch spec.

    Keep a delegation, or a new turn of a continuation, Pending while its
    model has reached its parallel limit, and release it first come first
    served.

    List the tasks of one parent session id, or of every parent session id,
    each with its task id, its agent, its task status, the time it was
    created, its start time when one is recorded and its queue place while it
    is Pending in this process, after running the start-up sweep again.

    Offer a session cancel for one parent session id, and list the tasks of one
    parent session id that are still Pending or live.

    Offer the completion notice of each settled task that its caller did not
    wait
    for, with the collect tool of that front end named in it, and the
    stop notice
    of a task that was stopped, to the front end that made its delegation,
    so
    that every parent adapter words them the same
    way and none keeps its own list of running tasks.

    Shut down by cancelling every task in the live task registry and recording
    each one that outlives the deadline as cancelled.

    Offer the guided setup to a parent adapter and to the command line, with
    the setup operations that give the model listing, the setup state with
    each model tier marked listed and marked with the file it comes from, the
    model check of each chosen model, the setup preview of a change set for a
    setup scope and its apply, and each *setup refusal* with a
    code
    and a one-line reason, so that every front end shows the same result. A
    setup refusal is any error of a setup operation: a change set error, the
    stale preview error, the write error, the load error, a refused change or
    a missing or unsupported child host. Offer the guide text to every
    parent adapter, so that an init that a person starts asks the same
    questions on every parent host.
  }

  decisions {
    A separate service keeps TaskCore to the lifecycle of one task, and gives
    the
    start-up sweep, the agent lookup and the live task registry one owner
    instead
    of one for each front end. The files and the expected output are rendered
    into
    the brief text because the child port carries one brief, and the fields
    exist
    to push a parent model toward a fuller brief. The reply budget is a field
    of
    the
    delegation and not a size of the service, because the size a parent model
    can
    take inline is known only to the front end that returns the result.

    A task that runs the fallback agent records its agent as fallback and not
    as
    general, because its tool list is its own and not that of the
    general agent. A
    task that runs the general agent without a model tier records its agent as
    general, whether the delegation names it or names nothing. A tiered
    task records
    its agent as <agent>:<tier>, so that listings, launch names and the frozen
    agent definition show which model tier ran, and the frozen definition with its
    model is
    the proof that the work was routed. The queue place is counted per process
    because the parallel limit is, so it names the place in this session's
    queue
    only.

    A continuation reaches only the tasks of its own parent session id, so
    that a parent model never sends a message into the work of another
    conversation, and a process that continues a task of that parent session
    after its first owner process exited, as a pi resume in a new pi process
    can, takes it over. Only a turn that is running needs its live owner,
    which alone holds the stdin of its child, so a settled or abandoned task
    can be taken over even while its owner lives. The rule holds on every
    parent host, because no core service names one. Continuing any task of
    the project is left for later.

    The status line and the result text live here, and not in a front end,
    because
    this component is the one owner of the state of a task: the wording of that
    state is shared by every front end, and a front end that imported it from
    another front end would depend on a parent adapter, which the Kernel
    forbids
    of every core service, the command line included. The queued view is
    derived here, so its wording sits beside it. Fitting a reply to the limit
    of
    its parent host and ordering a listing stay with the front end that needs
    them.

    Each built-in agent takes its starter tools from the child driver here,
    where the driver registry is, so that no component below AgentConfig names
    a tool. The guided setup lives here, beside the delegation, so that the
    Claude Code parent host, through the command line, and the pi parent host
    reach the
    same setup operations and cannot drift apart. The conversation of an init
    that a person starts, its questions, suggestions and warnings, lives in
    the guide text that
    both parent adapters follow, and not in the setup operations. A model check is
    a
    one-turn run of the child driver and not a delegation, because a
    delegation needs an agent entry and makes a task record.
  }
}
